{
  "response": "Let me analyze this React component for accessibility design issues. This is a classic \"div with click handler instead of native button\" anti-pattern, which I can identify even before reading the code in detail based on my pre-commitment predictions.\n\nLet me work through all phases of the investigation protocol systematically.\n\n## Phase 0: Consume Test Evidence\nNo test evidence provided (no axe-core results, no Playwright keyboard tests, no screen reader logs). Proceeding with design review only.\n\n## Phase 1: Pre-commitment Predictions\nBased on component type (expandable/disclosure widget):\n1. **Trigger is a div instead of button** - No native semantics, not focusable by default, no Enter/Space activation\n2. **Missing ARIA attributes** - No aria-expanded, no role=\"button\", no aria-controls\n3. **No keyboard support** - Space/Enter won't work on a div without explicit handlers\n4. **Visual indicator (+/\u2212) announced as text** - Screen readers will read \"plus\" or \"minus\" redundantly\n5. **Content not associated with trigger** - No aria-controls relationship\n\n## Phase 2: Semantic HTML Audit\nThe component uses `<div>` for the trigger element instead of a native `<button>`. This is a fundamental semantic error. The disclosure pattern should use either:\n- A native `<button>` (preferred) or\n- A `<div role=\"button\">` with all keyboard handling\n\nUsing a div means:\n- Not in tab order by default\n- No Enter/Space activation without explicit JS\n- No implicit button semantics for screen readers\n- No accessible name from text content automatically\n\n## Phase 3: ARIA Pattern Compliance Audit\nThe WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure (Show/Hide) pattern requires:\n- `role=\"button\"` on the trigger (if not using native `<button>`)\n- `aria-expanded` state (\"true\"/\"false\")\n- `aria-controls` referencing the content panel ID\n\nThis component has NONE of these. It's 0% ARIA compliant, not even 80%. The pattern is completely missing.\n\n## Phase 4: Focus Management Review\n- Trigger div is NOT focusable (no tabindex) - keyboard users cannot reach it at all\n- No focus indicator since the element isn't focusable\n- Content appears/disappears but no focus management for dynamic content\n- Tab order skips this entirely\n\n## Phase 5: State Communication Audit\n- `isExpanded` state exists in React but is NOT communicated to assistive technology\n- No aria-expanded attribute\n- Visual +/\u2212 indicator is text that screen readers will read as \"plus\" or \"minus\" - redundant and confusing\n- Content visibility changes (conditional render) with no announcement\n\n## Phase 6: Multi-Perspective Review\n\n**Screen reader user:**\n- Trigger div has no role, so it's announced as generic \"group\" or just the text content\n- No aria-expanded means state is not communicated\n- \"+/\u2212\" characters read aloud as \"plus\"/\"minus\" - confusing and redundant\n- Content appearing/disappearing without announcement\n\n**Keyboard-only user:**\n- Cannot tab to the trigger (divs aren't focusable)\n- Even if they could, Enter/Space wouldn't toggle it\n- Completely non-functional via keyboard\n\n**Low vision user:**\n- Visual styling might indicate clickability but no focus indicator for keyboard users\n- No high contrast issues specific to this component beyond lack of focus ring\n\n**Cognitive accessibility:**\n- \"+/\u2212\" symbols are somewhat clear visually but not programmatically labeled\n- Error handling: none needed here, but the interaction is unclear without proper semantics\n\n## Phase 7: Gap Analysis (What's Missing)\n1. **Missing native button element** - Should use `<button>` instead of `<div>`\n2. **Missing aria-expanded** - State not communicated to AT\n3. **Missing role=\"button\"** - If keeping div, needs explicit role\n4. **Missing keyboard event handlers** - No onKeyDown for Enter/Space (if using div)\n5. **Missing tabindex** - Trigger not focusable if using div\n6. **Missing aria-controls** - Content panel not referenced by trigger\n7. **Missing focus indicator styling** - No visible focus ring\n8. **Visual +/\u2212 text exposed to AT** - Should be aria-hidden or use CSS\n\n## Phase 8: Realist Check (Severity Calibration)\n- This is a CRITICAL issue because keyboard users cannot access the expandable content at all\n- Screen reader users get no state information and may not understand it's interactive\n- The fix is straightforward but the impact of shipping as-is is complete loss of functionality for non-mouse users\n\n## Phase 9: Self-Audit\nAll findings are HIGH confidence - they're based on clear violations of fundamental accessibility principles. No low-confidence items to move to Open Questions.\n\nLet me now structure my verdict and findings properly with file:line references (using the component code as reference).# Accessibility Design Review \u2014 BuggyExpandable Component\n\n## Phase 0: Consume Test Evidence\nNo automated test results, keyboard interaction traces, or screen reader logs were provided. This review is based on design analysis of the source code only. Findings would be strengthened by measured evidence from `accessibility-testing` and `a11y-test`.\n\n---\n\n## Pre-commitment Predictions (Phase 1)\n\nBefore reading the code in detail, I predicted these issues for a disclosure/expandable widget:\n1. **Trigger is a `<div>` instead of `<button>`** \u2014 not focusable by default, no native keyboard activation\n2. **Missing `aria-expanded`** \u2014 state not communicated to assistive technology\n3. **No keyboard support** \u2014 Space/Enter won't activate without explicit handlers on a div\n4. **`+` / `\u2212` characters exposed as text** \u2014 screen readers announce \"plus\"/\"minus\" redundantly\n5. **Content panel not associated via `aria-controls`** \u2014 no programmatic relationship between trigger and content\n\nAll five predictions were confirmed in the code. No surprises.\n\n---\n\n## Semantic HTML Audit (Phase 2)\n\nThe component uses a `<div>` as an interactive trigger element:\n\n```jsx\n<div className=\"expandable-trigger\" onClick={toggle}>\n  {title}\n  {isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}\n</div>\n```\n\n**CRITICAL \u2014 Native HTML semantics violated.** A disclosure widget's trigger must be a `<button>` (or at minimum `role=\"button\"` with full keyboard handling). Using a bare `<div>`:\n- Is not in the tab order by default \u2192 **keyboard users cannot reach it at all**\n- Has no implicit button role for screen readers \u2192 announced as generic text/group, not interactive\n- Does not activate on Enter/Space without explicit JavaScript handlers\n\nThis is exactly the anti-pattern flagged in the component's own \"Expected Behavior\" section: *\"Semantically correct button element\"* \u2014 yet the code uses `<div>`. The expected behavior was stated but never implemented.\n\n**WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard (Level A)** \u2014 all functionality must be available via keyboard.\n**WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A)** \u2014 elements must have proper semantics.\n\n---\n\n## ARIA Pattern Compliance Audit (Phase 3)\n\nPer the **WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices Guide \u2014 Disclosure (Show/Hide) pattern**, a disclosure widget requires:\n\n| Required Attribute | Present? |\n|---|---|\n| `role=\"button\"` on trigger (if not native `<button>`) | \u274c Missing |\n| `aria-expanded` (\"true\"/\"false\") on trigger | \u274c Missing |\n| `aria-controls` referencing content panel ID | \u274c Missing |\n\nThe component has **zero** of the three required ARIA attributes. The pattern is 0% implemented, not merely \"80% complete.\" There is no partial implementation \u2014 it's entirely absent.\n\nAdditionally, the `+` / `\u2212` characters are rendered as literal text nodes:\n```jsx\n{isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}\n```\nScreen readers will announce these as \"minus\" and \"plus\" respectively (WCAG 4.1.2). Since state should be communicated via `aria-expanded`, this visual-only indicator creates redundant, confusing announcements.\n\n**WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A)** \u2014 state must be programmatically determinable.\n**WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure Pattern** \u2014 requires `aria-expanded` and `aria-controls`.\n\n---\n\n## Focus Management Review (Phase 4)\n\n| Aspect | Status | Issue |\n|---|---|---|\n| Tab order | \u274c Broken | `<div>` is not focusable; keyboard users cannot reach the trigger at all |\n| Focus indicator | \u274c Absent | No `:focus` styling exists in CSS (not shown but implied by bare div) |\n| Keyboard activation | \u274c Missing | No `onKeyDown`/`onKeyPress` handler for Enter/Space |\n| Dynamic content focus | \u26a0\ufe0f Unmanaged | Content appears/disappears with no focus management or announcement |\n\nThe trigger element is completely unreachable via keyboard. This isn't a \"focus trap\" issue \u2014 it's worse: the interactive element doesn't exist in the tab order at all. A keyboard-only user would never discover this widget exists.\n\n**WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard (Level A)** \u2014 functionality must be available via keyboard interface.\n**WCAG 2.4.7 Focus Visible (Level AA)** \u2014 focus indicator must be visible.\n\n---\n\n## State Communication Audit (Phase 5)\n\n| State | Visual? | Programmatic? | Issue |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Expanded/Collapsed | \u2705 (+/\u2212 symbol changes) | \u274c No `aria-expanded` | Screen reader users never learn the state changed |\n| Content visibility | \u2705 Conditional render | \u26a0\ufe0f No announcement | Content appearing/disappearing is silent to AT |\n\nThe React state (`isExpanded`) exists but is **never communicated** to assistive technology. The only \"state indicator\" is a `+`/`\u2212` character that screen readers read as literal text (\"plus\"/\"minus\"), which:\n1. Is redundant if `aria-expanded` were properly set\n2. Provides no semantic meaning on its own (a user hearing \"plus\" doesn't know it means \"click to expand\")\n\n**WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A)** \u2014 state changes must be programmatically determinable.\n**WCAG 4.1.3 Status Messages (Level AA)** \u2014 dynamic content updates should be announced.\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Perspective Review (Phase 6)\n\n### Screen Reader User \u2014 \u26a0\ufe0f HIGH ALARM\n- Trigger `<div>` has no role \u2192 announced as generic text, not interactive\n- No `aria-expanded` \u2192 state change is completely silent\n- `+`/`\u2212` characters read aloud as \"plus\"/\"minus\" \u2192 confusing and redundant\n- Content panel appears/disappears with no announcement or association\n\n### Keyboard-Only User \u2014 \u26a0\ufe0f HIGH ALARM  \n- Trigger `<div>` is not in tab order \u2192 **completely unreachable**\n- No Enter/Space activation handlers \u2192 even if focused, nothing would happen\n- No focus indicator styling \u2192 can't see where they are (if they could get there)\n\n### Low Vision User \u2014 \u26a0\ufe0f MEDIUM ALARM\n- Visual `+`/`\u2212` indicator may be small or low-contrast\n- No `:focus` ring means keyboard users navigating elsewhere lose context when this element is involved\n- At 200% zoom, the clickable area (div with no explicit sizing) may become hard to target\n\n### Cognitive Accessibility \u2014 \u26a0\ufe0f MEDIUM ALARM\n- `+`/`\u2212` symbols are somewhat intuitive visually but lack programmatic labels for AT users\n- No clear indication that clicking toggles content (relies entirely on visual affordance of the div styling, which isn't visible in this code)\n- Error handling: not applicable here\n\n### Vestibular & Motion \u2014 LOW ALARM\nNo animations or motion effects present. Not relevant to this component.\n\n### Auditory Access \u2014 LOW ALARM  \nNo audio/video content. Not relevant.\n\n### Environmental Contrast \u2014 MEDIUM ALARM\nCannot verify contrast ratios from code alone, but the `+`/`\u2212` text indicator and trigger styling need verification against 4.5:1 (text) and 3:1 (UI boundary) thresholds.\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|---|---|---|\n| Screen reader | HIGH | No role, no aria-expanded, redundant +/- text |\n| Keyboard-only | HIGH | Div not focusable, no activation handlers |\n| Low vision | MEDIUM | No focus indicator, unverified contrast |\n| Cognitive | MEDIUM | Unclear affordance, symbols without labels |\n| Vestibular & motion | LOW | No animations present |\n| Auditory access | LOW | No media content |\n| Environmental contrast | MEDIUM | Contrast ratios not verifiable from code |\n\n---\n\n## Gap Analysis \u2014 What's Missing (Phase 7)\n\n1. **Missing native `<button>` element** \u2014 The trigger should be a `<button>`, not a `<div>`. This is the single most impactful fix.\n2. **Missing `aria-expanded`** \u2014 State must be communicated to assistive technology as `\"true\"` or `\"false\"`.\n3. **Missing `aria-controls`** \u2014 Must reference the content panel's ID to establish programmatic relationship.\n4. **Missing keyboard event handlers** \u2014 If keeping a div (not recommended), needs `onKeyDown` for Enter/Space and `tabIndex=\"0\"`. With `<button>`, this is automatic.\n5. **Missing focus indicator styling** \u2014 No `:focus-visible` or `:focus` CSS rules visible.\n6. **`+`/`\u2212` text exposed to AT** \u2014 Should be in an `aria-hidden=\"true\"` span, with state communicated via `aria-expanded`.\n7. **No content panel ID** \u2014 The conditionally-rendered `<div>` has no stable ID for `aria-controls` to reference (and it's unmounted when collapsed, which breaks the relationship anyway).\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### CRITICAL: Trigger is a non-interactive `<div>` instead of `<button>` \u2014 keyboard users cannot access this widget at all\n- **Evidence**: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:9-14` \u2014 `<div className=\"expandable-trigger\" onClick={toggle}>` with no `tabIndex`, no role, no keyboard handlers\n- **User group impacted**: Keyboard-only users (complete loss of function), screen reader users (no interactive semantics)\n- **Expected behavior**: Per WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern and WCAG 2.1.1, the trigger must be a native `<button>` element (or `role=\"button\"` with full keyboard handling). Native buttons are focusable by default and activate on Enter/Space automatically.\n- **Fix**: Replace `<div className=\"expandable-trigger\" onClick={toggle}>` with `<button type=\"button\" className=\"expandable-trigger\" onClick={toggle} aria-expanded={isExpanded}>`. This single change fixes tab order, keyboard activation, focus indication, and role semantics simultaneously.\n\n### CRITICAL: No `aria-expanded` state communicated to assistive technology\n- **Evidence**: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:9-14` \u2014 trigger has no ARIA attributes; React state `isExpanded` exists at line 5 but is never reflected in the DOM\n- **User group impacted**: Screen reader users (cannot determine expanded/collapsed state)\n- **Expected behavior**: Per WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern and WCAG 4.1.2, the trigger must have `aria-expanded=\"true\"` when content is visible and `\"false\"` when collapsed.\n- **Fix**: Add `aria-expanded={isExpanded}` to the button element.\n\n### CRITICAL: No `aria-controls` relationship between trigger and content panel\n- **Evidence**: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:9-14` (trigger) and `BuggyExpandable.jsx:16-18` (content `<div>`) \u2014 no ID on content, no `aria-controls` on trigger\n- **User group impacted**: Screen reader users (cannot navigate between trigger and controlled content)\n- **Expected behavior**: Per WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern, the trigger must have `aria-controls` referencing the content panel's ID. The content panel should also have a stable presence in the DOM (or at minimum an ID when rendered).\n- **Fix**: Give the content `<div>` an `id=\"expandable-content\"` and add `aria-controls=\"expandable-content\"` to the button. Consider keeping the content mounted with conditional visibility rather than unmounting, so the relationship persists.\n\n### MAJOR: `+`/`\u2212` characters exposed as text to screen readers\n- **Evidence**: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:12` \u2014 `{isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}` renders literal Unicode characters as text nodes\n- **User group impacted**: Screen reader users (hear \"plus\" or \"minus\" announced redundantly)\n- **Expected behavior**: Per WCAG 4.1.2, visual-only state indicators that duplicate programmatic state should be hidden from assistive technology using `aria-hidden=\"true\"`. The `+` / `\u2212` symbol is purely decorative once `aria-expanded` communicates the state.\n- **Fix**: Wrap the symbol in `<span aria-hidden=\"true\">{isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}</span>`.\n\n### MAJOR: Content panel unmounted when collapsed \u2014 breaks ARIA relationship and causes layout shift\n- **Evidence**: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:16` \u2014 `{isExpanded && (` conditionally renders/unmounts the content div entirely\n- **User group impacted**: Screen reader users (aria-controls target disappears), all users (layout reflow)\n- **Expected behavior**: Per WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern, the content panel should remain in the DOM with `hidden` attribute (or conditional visibility) rather than being unmounted. This maintains the `aria-controls` relationship and prevents layout shift.\n- **Fix**: Keep the `<div>` always mounted; use `hidden={!isExpanded}` or CSS-based show/hide instead of conditional rendering.\n\n### MINOR: No visible focus indicator styling for keyboard users\n- **Evidence**: Component code shows no `:focus` or `:focus-visible` CSS rules (CSS not provided but implied absent)\n- **User group impacted**: Keyboard-only users, low vision users\n- **Expected behavior**: Per WCAG 2.4.7 and 2.4.13, interactive elements must have a visible focus indicator with at least 2px perimeter outline and 3:1 contrast change against adjacent colors.\n- **Fix**: Add CSS `:focus-visible` styles to the trigger button (e.g., `outline: 2px solid Highlight; outline-offset: 2px;` or a custom high-contrast ring).\n\n---\n\n## What's Missing (Gaps)\n\n| Gap | Why It Matters |\n|---|---|\n| **No native `<button>` element** | The fundamental interactive semantics are missing \u2014 this is not an enhancement, it's the core requirement |\n| **No `aria-expanded`** | State changes are invisible to assistive technology users |\n| **No `aria-controls` + stable content ID** | No programmatic relationship between trigger and controlled content |\n| **Content unmounted on collapse** | Breaks ARIA relationships and causes layout reflow; should use `hidden` attribute instead |\n| **`+`/`\u2212` text exposed to AT** | Redundant/confusing announcements for screen reader users |\n| **No focus indicator CSS** | Keyboard users can't see where they are focused |\n| **No `type=\"button\"` on button** (if fixed) | Without it, the button defaults to `type=\"submit\"`, which could submit a parent form unexpectedly |\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n- **Screen reader user**: Would encounter a non-interactive `<div>` with no role. Hearing \"plus\" or \"minus\" announced as text provides zero semantic meaning about expand/collapse functionality. State changes are completely silent. Content appearing/disappearing is unannounced and unassociated.\n- **Keyboard-only user**: Cannot tab to the trigger at all (it's a `<div>` with no `tabIndex`). Even if they could focus it, Enter/Space wouldn't activate it because there are no keyboard event handlers. The widget is entirely non-functional via keyboard \u2014 this isn't a degraded experience, it's zero access.\n- **Low vision user**: No `:focus` indicator means keyboard navigation context is lost. At 200% zoom, the clickable area (a div with unknown dimensions) may be hard to target precisely. Contrast of `+`/`\u2212` text against background needs verification.\n- **Cognitive accessibility**: The `+`/`\u2212` symbols are somewhat intuitive visually but lack programmatic labels for AT users. There's no clear affordance indicating this is interactive \u2014 it relies entirely on CSS styling (not shown) that may or may not convey clickability.\n\n---\n\n## Verdict Justification\n\n**VERDICT: REJECT**\n\nThis component fails at the most fundamental level of accessibility design: **the trigger element uses a non-interactive `<div>` instead of a native `<button>`.** This is not an enhancement opportunity \u2014 it's a complete failure to implement basic interactive semantics. The \"Expected Behavior\" section in the fixture explicitly states *\"Semantically correct button element\"* and *\"Keyboard user can use Space/Enter to toggle,\"* yet neither requirement is met in the code.\n\nThe component has **zero** of the three required ARIA attributes for the WAI-ARIA Disclosure pattern (`aria-expanded`, `aria-controls`, `role=\"button\"`). The React state exists but is never communicated to assistive technology. Keyboard users cannot reach or activate the widget at all \u2014 this is complete loss of access, not degraded experience.\n\nThis would need a near-complete rewrite of the trigger element (replacing `<div>` with `<button>`, adding ARIA attributes, fixing content rendering strategy) before it could be considered acceptable. The fix itself is straightforward but the gap between stated expectations and actual implementation is total.\n\n**What would change this to ACCEPT:**\n1. Replace `<div className=\"expandable-trigger\">` with `<button type=\"button\" aria-expanded={isExpanded} aria-controls=\"content-id\">`\n2. Add stable `id` to content panel and keep it mounted (use `hidden` instead of conditional unmount)\n3. Wrap `+`/`\u2212` in `<span aria-hidden=\"true\">`\n4. Add `:focus-visible` CSS styling\n\n---\n\n## Open Questions (unscored)\n\n- **CSS not provided**: The component references className values (`expandable-section`, `expandable-trigger`, `expandable-content`) but no styles are shown. Are there existing focus indicator styles? Is the trigger visually styled to look clickable? These affect low vision and cognitive perspectives but cannot be verified from code alone.\n- **Content panel ID strategy**: If keeping content mounted with `hidden` attribute, what ID naming convention should be used for `aria-controls`? Should it support multiple instances on a page (requiring unique IDs)?\n- **`type=\"button\"` necessity**: Does this component render inside a `<form>`? If so, the button must have `type=\"button\"` to prevent accidental form submission. This is context-dependent and cannot be verified from the component in isolation.",
